<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Feature Prioritization Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Product teams often face competing requests, limited development
                    capacity, changing business priorities, and pressure from multiple
                    stakeholders. A structured prioritization process helps reduce
                    decision-making conflicts and keeps product development focused.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Too Many Feature Requests:</b> Categorize and evaluate requests using consistent customer and business criteria.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Stakeholder Conflicts:</b> Use transparent prioritization criteria and evidence to create alignment between teams.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Limited Development Capacity:</b> Focus resources on high-value features and avoid unnecessary scope expansion.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Changing Priorities:</b> Review the roadmap regularly and adjust priorities when meaningful business or customer changes occur.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Feature Bias:</b> Support decisions with customer research, product analytics, market information, and measurable evidence.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Ignoring Technical Dependencies:</b> Include technical complexity, architecture constraints, and dependencies during prioritization.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> <b>Difficulty Measuring Value:</b> Define expected outcomes and success metrics before development begins.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant challenges image here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Customer Value and Business Impact Analysis</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Feature prioritization should consider both customer and business
                    perspectives. A feature may have strong customer value but require
                    significant resources, while another may provide substantial business
                    benefits with relatively low implementation effort.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Identify the customer problem addressed by each feature.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Estimate the number and importance of customers affected by the feature.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Evaluate potential revenue, retention, adoption, or operational benefits.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Consider whether the feature supports important strategic objectives.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Compare expected value with implementation effort and risk.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant customer value image here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Prioritization Frameworks and Scoring</b></h3>

                <p>
                    A prioritization framework provides a consistent method for comparing
                    different product opportunities. Teams can evaluate features using
                    factors such as impact, confidence, effort, urgency, strategic value,
                    and customer importance.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Define the criteria that will be used to evaluate features.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Assign appropriate scores or ratings to each feature.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Compare expected impact against development effort and complexity.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Consider confidence levels when product information or customer evidence is limited.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Use the resulting ranking to guide roadmap and release decisions.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant prioritization framework image here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

